BT 1 0 0 1 14 362 Tm (1. Open with scope. Say clearly: "I am designing the feed backend, not all of Instagram. The scope is post) Tj T* ET
BT 1 0 0 1 14 349 Tm (creation, home feed, profile feed, and media delivery." 30 seconds.) Tj T* ET
BT 1 0 0 1 14 331 Tm (2. State the key insight immediately. This is a read-heavy system. Feed reads are 50x the write volume. The) Tj T* ET
BT 1 0 0 1 14 318 Tm (whole design exists to serve reads fast. Say this before drawing anything. 30 seconds.) Tj T* ET
BT 1 0 0 1 14 300 Tm (3. Draw the architecture. Client ) Tj /F3 9 Tf 10.8 TL (\256) Tj /F1 9 Tf 10.8 TL ( API Gateway ) Tj /F3 9 Tf 10.8 TL (\256) Tj /F1 9 Tf 10.8 TL ( Post/Feed/Follow Services ) Tj /F3 9 Tf 10.8 TL (\256) Tj /F1 9 Tf 10.8 TL ( Kafka ) Tj /F3 9 Tf 10.8 TL (\256) Tj /F1 9 Tf 10.8 TL ( Fanout Worker ) Tj /F3 9 Tf 10.8 TL (\256) Tj /F1 9 Tf 10.8 TL T* ET
BT 1 0 0 1 14 287 Tm (Feed Store / Redis. Show S3 + CDN for media. 2 minutes.) Tj T* ET
BT 1 0 0 1 14 269 Tm (4. Explain the fan-out strategy: this is the core of the question. "Fan-out on write for normal users, fan-out on) Tj T* ET
BT 1 0 0 1 14 256 Tm (read for celebrities, first page cached in Redis." One sentence per approach. 2 minutes.) Tj T* ET
BT 1 0 0 1 14 238 Tm (5. Walk through the post creation flow. Post saved + OutboxEvent in same transaction. Publisher sends to) Tj T* ET
BT 1 0 0 1 14 225 Tm (Kafka after commit. Fanout Worker distributes to followers. Feed Service reads from cache or Feed Store.) Tj T* ET
BT 1 0 0 1 14 212 Tm (Media from CDN. 2 minutes.) Tj T* ET
BT 1 0 0 1 14 194 Tm (6. Cover one failure. Post saved but Kafka event not sent: Transactional Outbox fixes it. This is more useful) Tj T* ET
BT 1 0 0 1 14 181 Tm (than listing five failures shallowly. 1 minute.) Tj T* ET
BT 1 0 0 1 14 163 Tm (7. Close with one tradeoff. Fan-out on write vs read, and why cursor pagination is mandatory at scale. 1) Tj T* ET
BT 1 0 0 1 14 150 Tm (minute.) Tj T* ET
